  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "bR3c+n5m7TnVhL11UIRHNfxxzpQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:07.894Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "PinkRoccade's healthcare and local-government units cover the two verticals the thesis targets most directly \u2014 the assessment calls them ideal \u2014 and the row is still filed do-not-pitch. The reason recorded is the model: it sells its own vertical products rather than assembling and integrating other people's components.\n\nThat inversion is the point worth keeping. Vertical fit describes where a firm's customers are; the delivery model describes whether a new layer can be introduced at all. A product vendor's answer to a missing audit trail is a roadmap item in its own software, and its customers buy an application, not an architecture they own. There is no seam for a third-party record layer to attach to, and any capability added there competes with the vendor's own release plan.\n\nThe record also carries a data-quality warning: the figures held for PinkRoccade are fifteen years old. Nothing size- or growth-dependent in the assessment can be trusted, and only the structural finding \u2014 product-led, in the right verticals \u2014 survives that staleness.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- PinkRoccade (Healthcare / Local Gov) (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Ideal verticals, wrong model. Figures 15 years old.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:07.894625Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:07.894630Z"
    }
  },
